================================================================================================================================================
Step 2 — Capacity Estimation:

            For an L6 system-design interview, capacity estimation is not about getting perfectly accurate numbers.
            
            The interviewer mainly wants to see whether you can:
            
                  1. Make reasonable assumptions.
                  2. Convert business numbers into technical load.
                  3. Identify the dominant workload.
                  4. Use those numbers to justify architectural decisions.
                  5. Recognize peak traffic and growth.
                  
            So let's do this exactly as you would in the interview.

2.1 Start with our assumptions

We established:

                  | Metric                       | Assumption |
                  | ---------------------------- | ---------: |
                  | Registered users             |       100M |
                  | Daily Active Users           |        10M |
                  | Requests per active user/day |         20 |
                  | Total requests/day           |       200M |
                  | Average RPS                  |      ~2.3K |
                  | Peak multiplier              |        10× |
                  | Peak RPS                     |       ~23K |


So our initial design target is approximately: 25K requests/second at peak

We can say in an interview:
                  "I'll design for roughly 25K peak requests per second based on a 10× peak-to-average multiplier.
                  The exact multiplier can be refined if the interviewer provides actual traffic patterns."

That is a good L6 answer because you're making the assumption explicit.


2.2 But 25K RPS is not enough

            This is where system-design interviews become interesting.
            
            We cannot simply say:            
                        "The system receives 25K RPS."
            
            We need to understand what those requests are doing.            
                        Suppose our 20 requests/user/day look roughly like this:
                                          10  → Product browsing
                                           4  → Search
                                           2  → Product details
                                           2  → Cart
                                           1  → Order/Checkout
                                           1  → Other

            So approximately:            
                        50% → Product browsing
                        20% → Search
                        10% → Product details
                        10% → Cart
                         5% → Checkout/order
                         5% → Other
            
            Now apply that to 25K peak RPS.

                        Product browsing  :   25K × 50%  ≈ 12.5K RPS
                        Search:  25K × 20% ≈ 5K RPS
                        Product details:  25K × 10% ≈ 2.5K RPS
                        Cart :  25K × 10% ≈ 2.5K RPS
                        Checkout/order : 25K × 5% ≈ 1.25K RPS

So we get: 
                                25K Peak RPS
                                     |
                   ┌─────────────────┼──────────────────┐
                   ↓                 ↓                  ↓
               Product/Search      Cart             Checkout
                   |
                   ↓
               ~20K RPS            ~2.5K             ~1.25K

      The exact distribution is an assumption, not a fact about Amazon/Flipkart.
      
      That's okay.

2.3 Why is this useful?:
                        Now something becomes obvious:
                        
                        Our system is overwhelmingly read-heavy.
                        
                        Approximately:
                                    READS  ≈ 80–90%
                                    WRITES ≈ 10–20%

            This is a huge architectural clue.
            
            Imagine we had designed:
                                          Client
                                            ↓
                                          Load Balancer
                                            ↓
                                          PostgreSQL

            Every product request would hit the database.
            
            At ~20K read requests/sec:
                                          20,000
                                             ↓
                                          PostgreSQL

            That creates unnecessary database pressure.
            
            Instead, we can start thinking:
                                            Product Request
                                                  ↓
                                               Cache
                                            ↙          ↘
                                        HIT             MISS
                                         ↓               ↓
                                     Response        Product DB

      This is the first point where capacity estimation directly leads to architecture.


2.4 Product data storage estimation:

            Now let's estimate product storage.
                        
                        Suppose:  100M products
                        Average product metadata: ~5 KB                        
                        Then: 100M × 5 KB
                        Approximately: 500 GB                        
                        So raw product metadata is roughly: 500 GB

But this doesn't include:
                        indexes
                        replicas
                        historical data
                        images
                        videos
                        search indexes
                        backups

Therefore actual infrastructure storage will be substantially larger.



2.5 Important distinction: product images:

                        Should we store images inside PostgreSQL?   Usually, no.
                        
                        Suppose a product has:  5 images                        
                        and each image averages: 500 KB                        
                        Then:  100M × 5 × 500 KB                        
                        That's:  250 TB
                        
                        Now imagine putting 250 TB of binary data inside a relational database.

            That's a very different workload.
            
            Instead:
                        Product metadata
                               ↓
                        Database
                        
                        Product images
                               ↓
                        Object Storage
                               ↓
                              CDN
                               ↓
                            Customer

      For example:
                        Product Page
                             |
                   ┌─────────┴─────────┐
                   ↓                   ↓
              Product API          Image URLs
                   ↓                   ↓
              Product DB              CDN
                                       ↓
                               Object Storage

      This is a classic example of separating metadata from large binary objects.


2.6 Why CDN?

            Imagine the same iPhone image is requested thousands of times.
            
            Without CDN:
                              10,000 users
                                   ↓
                              Application
                                   ↓
                              Object Storage

            With CDN:
                        10,000 users
                             ↓
                            CDN
                             ↓
                        Cache HIT


Only cache misses reach origin storage.

      Therefore CDN helps with:      
                  bandwidth reduction
                  latency
                  origin protection
                  traffic spikes
                  geographic distribution
      
      And notice the reasoning:
                              Large static objects
                                     +
                              High read volume
                                     ↓
                                    CDN

      We didn't choose CDN because:
                  "Every modern architecture needs a CDN."
      
      We chose it because the workload justifies it.

2.7 Order traffic

            Now let's look at the opposite side.
            
            Suppose peak checkout traffic is:  ~1,250 requests/sec
            
            This is much smaller than product reads.
            But here's the important point:            
                              Lower traffic doesn't mean lower importance.
            
            Order processing has much stricter correctness requirements.

For example:

            Product browsing: eventual consistency may be acceptable
            
            Order: incorrect state is unacceptable

            So we should not optimize everything purely for throughput.            
            We optimize each domain according to its business requirement.

2.8 Order storage:
                  Suppose we process:  5M orders/day                  
                  Assume an average order record + metadata is:  ~5 KB                  
                  Then:  5M × 5 KB  ≈ 25 GB/day                  
                  One year: 25 GB × 365 ≈ 9.1 TB

      That's raw logical data.
            Add:      
                  indexes
                  replicas
                  audit data
                  payment references
                  status history
                  backups

      and the physical storage requirement could easily become significantly larger.
      
      This immediately raises another question:      
                  Should we keep all historical orders in the primary transactional database forever?
      
      Not necessarily.  We could eventually introduce:

                                          Hot data
                                             ↓
                                          Primary OLTP database
                                          
                                          Older historical data
                                             ↓
                                          Archive / analytical storage

That's an example of data lifecycle management.
================================================================================================================================================
2.9 Kafka throughput

            Suppose every order generates approximately: 5 events

            Examples:            
                        OrderCreated
                        PaymentCompleted
                        InventoryReserved
                        OrderConfirmed
                        NotificationRequested
            
            At:                   5M orders/day
            we have:              5M × 5= 25M events/day            
            Average event rate:   25M / 86,400 ≈ 289 events/sec
            At peak, perhaps:    ~3K events/sec            
                                    depending on our peak assumptions.

So Kafka isn't necessarily required because we have massive event throughput.
It's useful because of decoupling and asynchronous processing.

That's a critical distinction.
================================================================================================================================================
2.10 Why Kafka if traffic isn't enormous?

            Imagine:           
                        Order Service
                              |
                              ├── Send email
                              ├── Update analytics
                              ├── Notify seller
                              ├── Update fulfillment
                              └── Send SMS
            
            If Order Service synchronously calls everything:
                                    Order
                                      ↓
                                    Email
                                      ↓
                                    SMS
                                      ↓
                                    Analytics
                                      ↓
                                    Fulfillment

            then one slow downstream service can slow the entire order request.
            
            Instead:
                        Order Service
                             |
                             ↓
                           Kafka
                             |
             ┌───────────────┼────────────────┐
             ↓               ↓                ↓
       Notification      Analytics       Fulfillment

Now consumers are decoupled.

      This gives us:      
                  asynchronous processing
                  buffering
                  retry
                  independent scaling
                  consumer isolation
                  replay capability

So:
      Kafka is primarily solving an architectural decoupling problem here, not merely a throughput problem.

That's exactly the kind of distinction I want you to be able to articulate in an L6 interview.
================================================================================================================================================
2.11 Cache estimation

                  Let's say we have:  100M products
                  
                  But we don't need every product in cache.

                  Suppose our top:   10M products   receive most of the traffic.
                  Average cached product representation:  5 KB
                  Then:   10M × 5 KB ≈ 50 GB
                  So we might need approximately:  50 GB logical cache capacity
                  
                  But real Redis deployment needs additional capacity for:                  
                                    replication
                                    metadata
                                    eviction headroom
                                    fragmentation
                                    failover

                  So we wouldn't provision exactly 50 GB.                  
                  This is another important interview principle:                  
                              Never provision infrastructure exactly at calculated capacity.
                  
                  You need headroom.
================================================================================================================================================
2.12 Our capacity picture

Now our system starts looking like this:

                                           CLIENTS
                                              |
                                              ↓
                                       ~25K peak RPS
                                              |
                                   ┌──────────┴──────────┐
                                   ↓                     ↓
                                READ PATH             WRITE PATH
                                   ↓                     ↓
                            ~20K+ RPS                  ~few K RPS
                                   |                     |
                             ┌─────┴─────┐          ┌────┴─────┐
                             ↓           ↓          ↓          ↓
                           Cache       Search     Cart       Order
                             |           |                     |
                             ↓           ↓                     ↓
                         Product DB  Search DB             Transaction DB

And static content:            
                        Images/Videos
                             ↓
                        Object Storage
                             ↓
                            CDN
            
Events:            
            Order
              ↓
            Kafka
              ↓
            Async consumers
            
 We're now ready to construct the actual high-level architecture.
================================================================================================================================================
2.13 One important correction to our thinking

      Before we move forward, remember this:
      
      Don't say:      
                  "We'll use Redis because the system needs to scale."
      
      Instead say:
      
                  "Product reads dominate our workload, and much of the product data is relatively stable. 
                  A distributed cache can absorb a large portion of those reads, 
                  reducing database load and improving latency."
      
      Similarly:
                  Don't say:                  
                              "We'll use Kafka because it's scalable."
                  
                  Say:                  
                        "Several downstream operations don't need to complete before the 
                        customer receives an order response. Kafka allows us to decouple 
                        those asynchronous workflows and provides buffering and independent consumer scaling."
                  
 This requirement → problem → solution → trade-off chain is one of the biggest things we'll train throughout this preparation.
