Step 3 — Search Product: End-to-End Request Flow

      Let's take one concrete requirement:
      
      A customer searches for "Nike running shoes".
      
      Our first target architecture will be:
                                              CUSTOMER
                                                  |
                                                  ↓
                                                 DNS
                                                  |
                                                  ↓
                                                 CDN
                                                  |
                                                  ↓
                                            Load Balancer
                                                  |
                                                  ↓
                                            API Gateway
                                                  |
                                                  ↓
                                           Search Service
                                                  |
                                                  ↓
                                        Search Index / OpenSearch
                                                  |
                                                  ↓
                                             Response
But don't accept this architecture yet.

We're going to derive every component.

================================================================================================================================================
3.1 Start with the user

            The customer enters:   Nike running shoes
            
            The client might call:  GET /api/v1/products/search?q=nike+running+shoes&page=1&size=20
            
            Conceptually:            
                              Mobile/Web
                                  |
                                  | HTTPS
                                  ↓
                              GET /products/search?q=nike running shoes
            
            Now the first question is: Where does this request go?

The client cannot simply know: 10.23.45.67

because our backend may have:
                              hundreds of servers
                              multiple regions
                              changing IP addresses
                              failover requirements

      So we need DNS.

3.2 DNS — What problem does it solve?

            Suppose our public domain is:  www.example-ecommerce.com
            
            The browser needs to convert:            
                                                www.example-ecommerce.com
                                                            ↓
                                                        IP address
            
That's DNS's basic job.
Conceptually:

                  Client
                     |
                     | "Where is www.example-ecommerce.com?"
                     ↓
                    DNS
                     |
                     ↓
                  IP / endpoint


Why not hard-code the server IP?  Because our infrastructure changes.

            Today:
                        Server A
                        10.1.1.10
            
            Tomorrow:
                        Server A removed
                        Server B added
                        Server C added
            
            The customer should not care.


 DNS gives us an abstraction:
                                    Stable domain
                                         ↓
                                    Changing infrastructure

============================================================================================================================================

3.3 L6 question: Is DNS a load balancer?

       This is a common interview trap.
                  
                  No—not in the same sense as an application/network load balancer.
                  
                  DNS can participate in traffic distribution, for example:

                                    example.com
                                         |
                                         ├── Region US
                                         ├── Region Europe
                                         └── Region Asia

      But DNS is fundamentally a name-resolution system.
      
      A load balancer operates on actual incoming network/application traffic.
      
      That's an important distinction.


================================================================================================================================================
3.4 DNS and multi-region architecture

Later, suppose we have:
                                    DNS
                                     |
                          ┌──────────┼──────────┐
                          ↓          ↓          ↓
                        India       US        Europe
                        Region     Region      Region


DNS-based routing can direct customers toward an appropriate region.

      But we don't need this complexity for our first version.
      
      For now:
                  Customer
                     ↓
                     DNS
                     ↓
                  Our application endpoint
================================================================================================================================================
3.5 Next: CDN:
                  Now here's an important question.                  
                  Should the search API response go through the CDN?                  
                  Be careful.                  
                              A CDN is excellent for:
                                                      Images
                                                      CSS
                                                      JavaScript
                                                      Videos
                                                      Static assets

                  For example:                  
                                    Product image
                                         ↓
                                    CDN
                                         ↓
                                    Customer
                                    
                  But search requests are dynamic: ?q=nike+running+shoes

      So we should not blindly say:  "Everything goes through CDN."
      
      Instead, we distinguish:  
                  Static content: → Excellent CDN candidates.
                                    Images
                                    CSS
                                    JS
                                    Product thumbnails


                  Dynamic APIs: → Usually handled by application infrastructure.
                                    Search
                                    Cart
                                    Checkout
                                    Order
                                    Payment

Some dynamic GET APIs can be cached at a CDN under carefully controlled conditions, but that's a separate optimization.
================================================================================================================================================
3.6 So why did we put CDN in our original diagram?

            Because an e-commerce platform has enormous amounts of static content.
            
            Imagine:
                        Nike shoe
                           |
                           ├── image1.jpg
                           ├── image2.jpg
                           ├── image3.jpg
                           └── image4.jpg

            Millions of users request these images.
            
            We want:           
                              User
                                ↓
                              CDN
                                ↓
                              Image
            
            rather than:            
                              User
                                ↓
                              Application
                                ↓
                              Object Storage
            for every request.
            
So our architecture is better represented as:
                                                     CUSTOMER
                                                         |
                                                  ┌──────┴──────┐
                                                  ↓             ↓
                                              Static         Dynamic
                                              Content         APIs
                                                  ↓             ↓
                                                 CDN           DNS
                                                  ↓             ↓
                                           Object Storage     Backend

This is a much more precise mental model.
